  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_hsm.c:408-433  __sbi_hsm_suspend_non_ret_save
      MSECCFG CSR is not saved across non-retentive suspend. The save function preserves MIE, MIP (SSIP|STIP only), MEDELEG, MIDELEG, and conditionally MENVCFG (priv >= 1.12), but MSECCFG — which contains MML (ePMP mode lock), RLB (rule lock bypass), SSEED/USEED (ZKR seed access) — is omitted. On resume, MSECCFG resets to the hardware default of 0, silently reverting ePMP enforcement to legacy PMP interpretation (MML=0 means PMP entries use legacy encoding) and disabling S-mode seed CSR access (SSEED=0). This is a direct violation: security-critical M-mode CSR state is lost across a hardware lifecycle transition with no compensating restore.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_init.c:483-499  init_warm_resume
      The warm resume path calls sbi_hart_reinit() (which only reinitializes mstatus and FP state) then sbi_hart_protection_configure(). sbi_hart_reinit() does NOT restore MSECCFG. When sbi_hart_protection_configure() dispatches to sbi_hart_smepmp_configure() which writes CSR_MSECCFG (RLB at line 139, MML at line 185), it operates on a core where MSECCFG has been reset to its hardware default of 0. The MSECCFG.MML=0 state causes the ePMP-encoded entries programmed immediately before (lines 144-180) to be interpreted as legacy PMP entries, silently breaking the security model. This is the direct downstream consequence of location #1's missing MSECCFG save.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_pmp.c:121-218  sbi_hart_smepmp_configure
      Writes to CSR_MSECCFG (RLB at line 139, MML at line 185) execute without in-function re-verification that SBI_HART_EXT_SMEPMP is still set. The capability check occurs only at registration time in sbi_hart_pmp_init() (line 335), which gates whether epmp_protection is registered at all. Once registered, sbi_hart_smepmp_configure() can be dispatched via the protection framework without any local capability check. Currently safe because extension flags are write-once at cold boot and never cleared, but this is an implicit invariant not enforced in code. After suspend/resume, MSECCFG has been reset (location #1) so even though the extension flag is still set, the CSR writes target hardware in an unexpected state (MML already 0, which is technically valid but means the prior ePMP configuration was silently lost).
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:102-168  switch_to_next_domain_context
      Base S-mode CSRs (sstatus, sie, stvec, sscratch, sepc, scause, stval, sip, satp) are saved/restored unconditionally at lines 131-139 without a misa_extension('S') check. Optional CSRs (scounteren, senvcfg, srmcfg) are conditionally saved/restored with explicit capability checks (priv version thresholds or extension flags). This pattern is correct: base S-mode CSRs are architecturally guaranteed to exist when misa.S=1, so no check is needed. Optional CSRs require runtime verification. The apparent 'inconsistency' is actually a deliberate and correct defensive programming pattern — checking for optional CSRs while trusting architecturally mandatory ones.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart_protection.c:52-62  sbi_hart_protection_configure
      Dispatches to the 'best' hart protection via sbi_hart_protection_best() without re-verifying that the chosen protection's hardware capabilities are still present at configuration time. The protection rating (ePMP=200, PMP=100) is established at registration during cold boot (sbi_hart_pmp_init). When called from the warm resume path (init_warm_resume), no runtime validation confirms that the hardware state matches what the registered protection expects. Currently, the extension flag check at registration is a reliable gate (flags are immutable after cold boot), but the hardware CSR state that the protection depends on (MSECCFG) has been silently reset by suspend (location #1). The dispatch itself is correct — it selects the right protection — but the protection's configure() function then executes on hardware whose state does not match the assumptions made at registration time.
